{0 authoring-estimators Authoring a third-party estimator}

ModelKit extensions are ordinary OCaml modules. An estimator package depends on
[modelkit], implements a public protocol, and packages an immutable
specification when it enters a pipeline. It does not depend on ModelKit's
private [Modelkit_*] compilation units or on an execution backend.

{1 Choose the protocol}

Implement {!module-type:Modelkit.ESTIMATOR} when fitting needs only features,
targets, an optional sample-weight argument, and an explicit RNG. Implement
{!module-type:Modelkit.METADATA_ESTIMATOR} when fitting requests typed metadata
such as weights, groups, or a progress callback. Classification implementations
use integer {!type:Modelkit.Target.classification} targets and regression
implementations use float64 {!type:Modelkit.Target.regression} targets.

In either protocol:

- [t] is an immutable training specification and [params] is its inspectable
  parameter representation.
- [fitted] is a distinct type containing learned values and the admitted
  {!Modelkit.Feature_schema.t}.
- [clone] preserves the specification's parameters; it does not copy fitted
  state.
- [fit] and [predict] return typed {!Modelkit.Error.t} values for routine data,
  shape, validation, numerical, and compatibility failures.
- every operation validates its schema and row-aligned inputs, and a fixed RNG
  stream produces deterministic results.

A metadata-aware regressor declares its needs before fitting:

{[
let fit_request _specification =
  Modelkit.Metadata.Request.create
    ~sample_weight:Modelkit.Metadata.Request.Required
    ~groups:Modelkit.Metadata.Request.Optional
    ~callback:Modelkit.Metadata.Request.Optional
    ()
]}

The pipeline validates all requests before any component fits and passes only
the requested fields to the implementation. The estimator must still validate
values it interprets. It must not retain fit metadata as an implicit input to a
later prediction.

{1 Package and identify the estimator}

Create provenance once from nonblank package, version, and implementation
identifiers, then pass it when packaging the specification:

{[
let package specification =
  let ( let* ) = Result.bind in
  let* provenance =
    Modelkit.Pipeline.provenance
      ~package:"acme-models"
      ~version:"1.2.0"
      ~implementation:"Robust_regressor"
  in
  Modelkit.Pipeline.metadata_estimator
    ~provenance
    ~name:"robust"
    (module Robust_regressor)
    specification
]}

Use {!val:Modelkit.Pipeline.estimator} for the ordinary estimator protocol. The
result enters {!Modelkit.Pipeline.set_estimator} and thereafter uses the same
pipeline, nested composition, cross-validation, search, and optional parallel
execution APIs as a built-in estimator. Search builders should construct a new
immutable specification for every candidate and invoke the same packaging
function.

Execution is owned by the workflow. A component should not create domains or
thread pools internally. Applications opt into bounded fold or candidate
execution by installing [modelkit-parallel] and passing its
[Modelkit_parallel.execution] value to cross-validation or search.

{1 Publish conformance checks}

Package tests should run {!Modelkit.Conformance.Estimator.check} or
{!Modelkit.Conformance.Metadata_estimator.check} against representative valid
data. The fixture supplies the observations that generic code cannot infer from
abstract target and prediction types: parameter equality, prediction length,
and deterministic prediction equality. Assert
{!Modelkit.Conformance.passed}; use {!Modelkit.Conformance.failures} and
{!Modelkit.Conformance.issue_to_string} for test-runner diagnostics.

Conformance is a protocol baseline, not a numerical validation suite. Component
authors remain responsible for invalid-input, numerical-edge, parity,
determinism, and platform tests appropriate to their algorithm. At least one
integration test should exercise the packaged estimator through the workflows
the package claims to support.

{1 Provenance and portable artifacts}

After fitting, {!val:Modelkit.Pipeline.artifact_report} lists every fitted stage
and the terminal estimator. Each component reports its optional provenance and
{!Modelkit.Pipeline.Portable_artifact}, {!Modelkit.Pipeline.External_artifact}, or
{!Modelkit.Pipeline.Unsupported}. Provenance is descriptive identity; it never
turns arbitrary behavior into a portable codec.

Ordinary public estimator adapters attach no codec. They remain fully
usable for in-memory fitting and prediction, while
{!Modelkit.Artifact.encode_regression} or its classification counterpart returns
a typed artifact error. ModelKit artifacts contain data rather than closures or
marshalled OCaml values. An external codec therefore requires a separately
reviewed, package-qualified, versioned data format and is not provided by an
ambient registry.

For a reviewed terminal estimator implementing {!Modelkit.REGRESSOR}, bind its
data-only IO functions to {!Modelkit.Artifact.Codec.regression}:

{[
let codec =
  Modelkit.Artifact.Codec.regression
    ~package:"acme-models" ~name:"Forecast" ~version:1
    (module Forecast)
    ~encode:Forecast.encode_model
    ~decode:Forecast.decode_model
  |> Result.get_ok

let package specification =
  let ( let* ) = Result.bind in
  let* provenance = Modelkit.Pipeline.provenance
      ~package:"acme-models" ~version:"1.2.0" ~implementation:"Forecast" in
  Modelkit.Artifact.Codec.estimator ~name:"forecast" ~provenance codec specification

let load bytes =
  Modelkit.Artifact.decode_regression
    ~decoders:[| Modelkit.Artifact.Codec.decoder codec |] bytes
]}

[Forecast.decode_model] receives [~provenance] and [~feature_schema] before its
payload argument. It validates its own payload and restores fitted state
without training data or refitting. Native decoders must validate supported
runtime versions and model/schema consistency and release acquired resources
on errors or exceptions. Inference configuration can be captured when creating
the codec used for decoding. Package identifiers and codec names are nonempty
ASCII letters, digits, hyphens, underscores, or dots; [modelkit] is reserved.
The integer codec version is independent of the component's package version.

{!Modelkit.Artifact.Codec.classification} similarly binds a classifier and its
class-label callback, with optional probability and decision dispatch. Its
decoder additionally receives a bounded, distinct, ordered [~classes] array
and must restore that exact mapping. A generalized classification envelope can
hold one or more classes; estimator-specific target restrictions remain the
estimator's responsibility. The binary-specific APIs require exactly two.

The decoder list is local to the load call. Missing or duplicate selections,
unknown codec versions, malformed framing/checksums, invalid schemas/classes,
and task mismatches fail with typed errors. The core validates the entire
envelope before an external callback. It does not interpret opaque external
payloads or extend its built-in safe-reader guarantee to them. Applications
select trusted codec code and follow its artifact trust policy; a checksum
detects corruption and does not establish authenticity.

{!Modelkit.Pipeline.artifact_supported} accepts both built-in and explicitly
attached external codecs; {!Modelkit.Pipeline.portable_artifact_supported}
accepts only built-in portable codecs. The current extension supports external
terminal estimators surrounded by codec-supported built-in preprocessing.
External transformer codecs, metadata-aware terminal codecs, and nested
composition remain unsupported and fail by component name.

Existing built-in regression and binary writers retain container version one.
External terminals use version two, which records package-qualified codec
identity, exact format version, producer provenance, and classification labels.
{!Modelkit.Artifact.encode_classification} always writes the generalized task in
version two. {!Modelkit.Artifact.decode_classification} accepts that task and
legacy binary artifacts; use it for generalized artifacts even when they have
two classes. The binary loader continues to require the binary task tag.

The separately compiled [test/public_codec_consumer] library uses public APIs
only to demonstrate weighted pipeline round trips, explicit version selection,
class-order preservation, bounded-reader failures, and committed version-two
golden artifacts.

The repository's separately built [test/public_consumer] library is the
executable reference for this guide. Its one metadata-aware estimator module is
used unchanged by conformance checks, nested composition, two-domain
cross-validation, randomized search, and artifact-support reporting while
depending only on the public [Modelkit] namespace.
